# Third-party extensions kept locally for reference only.
# These are Chrome Web Store downloads (someone else's copyrighted code,
# and in ChromeLock's case ~9 MB of deliberate obfuscation). Not ours to commit.
examples/*

# ...but keep our own analysis of them.
!examples/*/
examples/*/*
!examples/*/architecture.md
!examples/comparison.md

node_modules/
dist/
*.zip
*.crx
*.pem
.DS_Store

# ...except the published one. docs/ is what GitHub Pages serves, so the crx
# there is the artifact Chrome actually downloads: it has to be committed, and
# having it in the history is the point — that is what "tag it, keep the CRX"
# (architecture.md §10.8) means in practice.
!docs/*.crx

# The signing key *is* the extension id: lose it and the force-install policy
# points at nothing; leak it and someone else can sign an update Chrome accepts.
# It belongs in the password manager next to the master password, not in git.
keys/

# An exported escrow bundle holds no plaintext secret — its private half is
# sealed under the master password — but there is no reason to publish one.
escrow*.json

